  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      "Some Greek letters look exactly like English ones but stand for a different sound. They are the trickiest: an English reader sees *ΡΗΧ* and wants to say *p-h-x*, but it is *r-i-ch*. Learn these six well.",
      {
        "h": "False friends"
      },
      {
        "letters": [
          {
            "letter": "Β β",
            "latin": "v",
            "say": "like the v in van, never a b",
            "word": "βιβλίο",
            "word_latin": "vivlío",
            "meaning": "book"
          },
          {
            "letter": "Η η",
            "latin": "i",
            "say": "like the ee in see, never an h",
            "word": "ήλιος",
            "word_latin": "ílios",
            "meaning": "sun"
          },
          {
            "letter": "Ρ ρ",
            "latin": "r",
            "say": "a rolled r, as in Italian; never a p",
            "word": "νερό",
            "word_latin": "neró",
            "meaning": "water"
          },
          {
            "letter": "Υ υ",
            "latin": "i",
            "say": "also like the ee in see, never a u or a y",
            "word": "μύτη",
            "word_latin": "míti",
            "meaning": "nose"
          },
          {
            "letter": "Χ χ",
            "latin": "ch",
            "say": "like the ch in Scottish loch; softer, like the h in huge, before e and i",
            "word": "χέρι",
            "word_latin": "chéri",
            "meaning": "hand"
          },
          {
            "letter": "Ω ω",
            "latin": "o",
            "say": "like the o in for: the same sound as ο",
            "word": "ώρα",
            "word_latin": "óra",
            "meaning": "hour"
          }
        ],
        "lang": "el"
      },
      {
        "h": "One sound, several letters"
      },
      "Greek has three letters for the sound *i* and two for the sound *o*. They are read in exactly the same way: the difference is only in the spelling, as in English *meat* and *meet*. So when you see a word you can always read it, but when you hear it you have to learn how it is written.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "sound",
            "letters",
            "examples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "i",
              "ι, η, υ",
              "*σπίτι, ήλιος, μύτη*"
            ],
            [
              "o",
              "ο, ω",
              "*κιλό, ώρα*"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "The other capitals you have met, Α Ε Ι Κ Μ Ν Ο Τ, look like English capitals and also sound like them. It is the small letters that change shape: ν (n), η (i), ρ (r), υ (i), ω (o).",
      {
        "h": "Try reading"
      },
      "Read each word aloud, then tap it to check. With the letters of the first two lessons you can already say good morning: *καλημέρα*!",
      {
        "flash": [
          [
            "καλημέρα",
            "kaliméra",
            "good morning"
          ],
          [
            "νερό",
            "neró",
            "water"
          ],
          [
            "ώρα",
            "óra",
            "hour, time"
          ],
          [
            "ήλιος",
            "ílios",
            "sun"
          ],
          [
            "χέρι",
